Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An act is considered to be “non-voluntary” when an agent acts out of ignorance, but does not feel regret for the action afterwards.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The distinction between “non-voluntary” actions and “involuntary” actions are slim, and this distinction made by Aristotle is often confused.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A non-voluntary action would be if, for example, an agent were to step on someone else’s toes without realizing it, but then showing no reaction at all. Aristotle considers this to be neither morally repugnant (since the agent did not commit the action willingly), but nor does he consider such an action morally praiseworthy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>On the other hand, an action is praise worthy or blameworthy if it is done voluntarily (and if the action concerns virtue or vice).</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a><ul><li>An action is never praise worthy nor blameworthy when it id done involuntarily.</li></ul></li></ul>","Bible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An important religion text that sets the foundation for religions like Christianity, Judaism, and Islam.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Understanding the bible is crucial for understanding many works of Western literature, since much of the writing done after the bible is a response to it or builds upon it.</p><h3>Genesis</h3><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Man is created in the image of God\">Man is created in the image of God</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not good for man to be alone\">It is not good for man to be alone</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a><ul><li>Suffering begins with original sin.<ul><li>In deflecting his responsibility of eating from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, Adam cuts off his relationship both to the divine and to the human, since he blames Eve, and in extension, since he claims that Eve is the creation of God, he blames God.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Angel to Hagar</p><p>And the LORD’s messenger said to her, “<strong>Return to your mistress and suffer abuse at her hand.</strong>”… “For the LORD has <strong>heeded your suffering.</strong>”</p></blockquote><ul><li>Hagar is able to “see” the well after she is reminded by the Angel and remembers that Ismael will rule over a great nation.<ul><li>This is basically saying: “yes, one will suffer, but with faith in God, suffering is endurable and not as bad.”</li></ul></li><li>The Binding of Isaac:<ul><li>God’s direct command overrides the other moral precepts that have been laid down by him?<ul><li>Shows God as the creator of morality.</li></ul></li><li>This scene is not only a test of Abraham’s loyalty to God, but also a test of Isaac, and his loyalty to God and Abraham.</li></ul></li><li>The tree of knowledge of good and evil was there<ul><li>To have humans exercise free will</li><li>So that they could eat from it later?</li></ul></li><li>There is a contrast between the Jacob and Esau story and the Cain and Abel story:<ul><li>In the case of the former, the two brothers, although one tries to kill another, the brothers are “enlightened.” Esau does not kill Jacob, and Jacob in a way repays Esau for the trouble that he has caused him.</li><li>In the case of Cain and Abel, Cain successfully kills Abel, and there is no resolution between the two.</li></ul></li><li>In the Joseph story, there is a cycle of betrayal that is broken when Judah offers up himself instead of Benjamin to Joseph as a slave.<ul><li>Joseph was originally betrayed by his brothers since he was his father’s favorite.</li><li>Later, when the brothers arrive in Egypt, Joseph makes them play out what had happened to him with Jacob’s second son by Rachel, Benjamin. </li><li>When Judah offers to sacrifice himself rather than having for the brothers to sacrifice Benjamin, the cycle of sibling betrayal is finally ended, and Joseph reveals himself to his brothers.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Meta</h4><ul><li>The use of repetition in the bible is not an error, but rather intention, to ensure that the reader understands the circumstances and fully context of an event, to highlight the relationship of people to each other in the context of their current actions.</li><li>The betrothal scene between two characters is often indicative of the nature of their marriage.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Buddhahood is achieved through Sudden Enlightenment, which is the moment when one becomes one with the <em>wu</em>, or the Universal Mind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a theory of Chinese Buddhism, specifically, the theories developed by Zhu Daosheng.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>All  sentient beings are born with the Universal Mind, though they might not realize it. It is this blindness that prevents one from being able to escape the eternal reincarnation that occur as a result of <em>karma</em>. Eventually, through practice, one will be able to see one’s Universal Mind, at which point one is suddenly enlightened. That is also the moment when one realizes the true nature of the <em>wu</em>.</p><h4>References</h4>","Capitalism and Freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Friedman’s magnum opus, a piece about how political freedom is dependent on economic freedom.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the major works in economics.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The historical development of strong state institutions in China allows for highly centralized state action, though at the cost of individual freedom. The lack of such development in India has led to comparatively greater power to the masses and the development of the world’s largest democracy, though the government suffers from debilitating bureaucratic hurdles, local autonomy, and corruption.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This comparison between the two most populous states in the world essentially illustrate the two manner in which states can come to develop: either with strong or weak state institutions, with either of these paths leading to radically different outcomes.</p><h4>Historical Examples</h4><p>The rise of the Chinese state begins with the Legalistic Qin state, which was the first to unite all of China.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that although modern day China is highly centralized, it is up to debate whether this is due to some innate attribute of the way that Chinese state institutions developed, or due to some geographic quirk, or something else. I think that China’s centralization is in part due to its long history, its relative power compared to surrounding states (due to its geography), and the evolution of a national identity. So, because China’s state institutions have had a long time to develop, they were able to slowly establish frameworks for centralizing the state. Although there are periods of instability of fracturing, ultimately, the goal of any fracture state is not to remain independent, but “reclaim China.” Likewise, its relative lack of outsider influence means that it has been able to remain relative isolated and develop a unified identity. Today, modern technology has made it a lot easier to run a highly centralized state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India\">Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India</a><ul><li>This could partly explain the weak state in India.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>The way that the state does not collapse in on itself is through its channels of meritocracy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>This continues to be the case in China, and is perhaps made worse by the strong state control.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>China’s geography influenced the strengthen of its state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Xi Jinping seeks to establish a revolutionary spirit to maintain ideological purity and prevent institutional decay\">Xi Jinping seeks to establish a revolutionary spirit to maintain ideological purity and prevent institutional decay</a><ul><li>This is an example of the strong state, since Xi is actually trying and maybe able to cultivate a revolutionary spirit.</li></ul></li></ul>","Contractarian reasoning appears to license compulsory bodily sacrifice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Parties choosing behind a veil of ignorance, not knowing whether they will need an organ, seem bound to agree to a scheme of compulsory donation, and since almost nobody accepts that conclusion, the reasoning that generates it is under suspicion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a reductio aimed at the original position itself rather than at any particular principle chosen within it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mankiw draws the further inference: if we hold rights over our own bodies against what the veil would license, it is unclear why we do not equally hold rights over the product of our labour. The available reply is that bodily integrity and income differ in that only the latter is produced by a cooperative scheme, which is the point made in the reciprocity argument.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>The reply that distinguishes income from bodily integrity.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position\">Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position</a><ul><li>The assumption that generates the objectionable agreement.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>The methodological commitment under attack.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cultures and institutions are different":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference between culture and institutions is threefold: culture arises naturally, while institutions are formed through deliberately constructed systems; cultures unconsciously shape people, while institutions function through formal rules; and cultures are hard to transplant, while institutions can be copied or imposed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial, because the difference between the two concepts is often forgotten, or ignored. Understanding the difference between the two concepts is also crucial for understanding Huntington’s thesis regarding Cultural Determinism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Cultures can influence institutions, but the way institutions develop is not solely determined by culture.<ul><li>The same cultural background can develop into vastly different institutions.<ul><li>Calvinism: pillarization in the Dutch Republic, autocracy in the Prussian state, and Apartheid in South America.</li></ul></li><li>The Athenian institution of democracy has spread and become a global phenomenon, but Athenian culture has not.<ul><li>Democracy was also not something that emerged out of Athenian culture. It emerged as a reaction against Athenian culture at the time.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development\">Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development</a></li></ul>","Dominant-end moral theories fail":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A dominant-end theory is an approach to rational deliberation whereby the result is one overarching goal, with all other ends being merely subordinate means to that ultimate end. Rawls argues that this view is impossible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument against dominant-end theories in general.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls makes three key points:</p><ol><li>There is no plausible option for the actual dominant end.<ul><li>Happiness cannot be it since happiness is the result of executing a rational plan of life (see the Thin Theory of the Good).</li><li>Many other proposals seem inhuman if they are presented as the overarching goal of human life.</li></ul></li><li>The vagueness of a dominant-end theory means that it does not actually solve the problem that it claims to. For instance, if happiness if the dominant end, then how does one define happiness?</li><li>Human good seems to be heterogeneous, and not convergent upon some ultimate, singular end.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a><ul><li>This unrealistic assumption is related to Rawls’ third objection here, and is part of the reason why it is unrealistic.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>The critiques here are related to Rawls’ 2nd critique in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When there is high levels of inflation, nominal interest rates rise, which means that costs for businesses and individuals seem to also rise and become less predictable, which leads to less investment overall.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument against high inflation, since increased uncertainty leads to less investment in the economy, which, in the long term, depresses economic growth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>There is uncertainty regarding the effect of uncertainty on the economy in the short/long run.</li></ul></li></ul>","Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People are defined more by what they are not than by what they are; what they are not is more important to politics than what they are.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a view of looking at politics and the way that politics happens different from the typical view.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Ideology is no longer the most important part about politics. </li><li>Rather, it is culture: ancestry, religion, language, history, values, customs, and institutions.</li><li>And with regarding these specific factors, people identify against the people who different from them in these specific factors, rather than specific identifying with people who share the same factors.</li></ul><h4>Christianity</h4><p>The same concept can be applied to the history of Christianity. Christianity has languished since there is no genuine “paganism” out there in order to sharpen and shape Christianity today. Contemporary Christianity has been so diluted and adulterated by secular influences and actions that it is not the Christianity that it once was.</p><ul><li>Joachim served the function of reinvigorating Christianity by challenges the status quo.</li><li>Other figures, such as St. Paul, Tertullian and St. Augustine, and Pascal have all served to preserve Christianity through their defense of what is “Christian,” though that term is so diluted now that it would be like punching at air.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Things get their meaning and identity from their relations within a structure</span><ul><li>This structuralist idea is supported here with Huntington's claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one\">Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one</a><ul><li>Again supported by/related to this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion\">Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion</a> <ul><li>Related to the discussion of Christianity here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Islamic states are far more likely to engage in violence than non-Islamic ones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It has been observed than Muslim states are more likely to engage in violence than non-Muslims ones, both towards other Muslim countries and non-Muslims countries.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could explain the relatively higher amount of conflicts between Muslims, other Muslims, and non-Muslims.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are many possible reasons for this being the case that have been proposed:</p><ul><li>Historical culture which glorified conquest for Muslims.</li><li>Due to their historical conquests, Muslims often live in close proximity to people of different cultures.</li><li>Muslim culture is less tolerant of non-Muslims, drawing a clear distinction between <em>Dar Al-Islam</em> and <em>Dar Al-harb</em>.</li><li>Muslims victimization by the West (although this would not explain Muslim conflicts against non-Muslim minorities).</li><li>The absence of a core state in the Muslim world.</li><li>Youth bulge, meaning there is an excess in the amount of population of fighting age.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a><ul><li>This could be part of the reason for the conflict, since conflict based on religion is common.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest\">Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest</a><ul><li>Although they might have spread through trade, violence is now erupting.</li></ul></li></ul>","It is not always good to know the truth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that someone, while searching for something, lead themselves to their own doom.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in many works of art and literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h5>King Hamlet</h5><ul><li>Hamlet’s primary motivation throughout the entirety of the play is searching for truth, to the extent that one can pin truth as the bedrock of his motivation to do anything at all. At the same time, his enthusiasm for truth is not met in part by his actual actions, as he does not actually act on his true beliefs.</li><li>This is relevant for understanding Hamlet, the main character of Hamlet the play. By understanding what drives Hamlet, we are better able to understand the reasons for the plot functioning in the way that it does.</li></ul><h5>Victor Frankenstein (Robert Walton)</h5><ul><li>Both Victor and Robert’s desires for truth are what undergirds the adventures that they go on.</li><li>Victor’s search for the means of creating life, to become the “modern Prometheus,” is eventually what leads to his downfall, as he creates a monster. He also dies in his journey to kill this creature that he creates.</li><li>Robert Walton’s desire to explore the North Pole is similar. He is driven by a desire to advance human knowledge, and would have continued on his quest if not for Victor’s tale.</li><li>The creature also expresses this sentiment: <ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I cannot describe to you the agony that these reflections inflicted upon me: I tried to dispel them, but sorrow only increased with knowledge. Oh, that I had forever remained in my native wood, nor known nor felt beyond the sensations of hunger, thirst and heat!</span><ul><li>While meeting humanity gave him access to many different experiences that are positive, he also found out about his true nature as a hideous creature, something that, had he never met humanity, he would never have found out.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Increase of knowledge only discovered to me more clearly what a wretched outcast I was.</span></li></ul></li></ul><h5>Oedipus</h5><ul><li>Oedipus searches for the truth through the entirety of the play, and the same search for the truth is also what ultimately brings him down.</li><li>This dedication to the truth is what eventually brings Oedipus down, as he find out about the truth regarding the relationship between him and his wife/mother, and the fact that he kills his father.</li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">TIRESIAS: None of you knows— and I will never reveal my dreadful secrets, not to say your own. OEDIPUS: What? You know and you won't tell? You're bent on betraying us, destroying Thebes?</span><ul><li>Here, it is the case of Tiresias having the truth. Yet, due to having the truth,  he is yelled at and blamed by Oedipus.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">OEDIPUS: Who primed you for this? Not your prophet's trade. TIRESIAS: You did, you forced me, twisted it out of me.</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">OEDIPUS: What—give up now, with a clue like this? Fail to solve the mystery of my birth? Not for all the world! JOCASTA: Stop—in the name of god, if you love your own life, call off this search! My suffering is enough.</span><ul><li>Jocasta, having heard that Oedipus was not the true son of the Corinthian king and queen, and having heard that he was given to them by a shepherd, begins the realize the truth and begs Oedipus to stop his search. Yet, Oedipus is adamant about finding out the terrible truth.</li></ul></li></ul><h5>Jack Burden</h5><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, p. 590</p><p>So I went back down and stood in the garden among the black magnolia trees and the myrtles, and thought how by killing my father I had saved my mother's soul. Then I thought how maybe I had saved my father's soul too. Both of them had found out what they needed to know to be saved. <strong>Then I thought how all knowledge that is worth anything is maybe paid for by blood. Maybe that is the only way you can tell that a certain piece of knowledge is worth anything: it has cost some blood.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>The truth is not something that can be wield and sought and acquired willy-nilly. It’s something that people will defend with blood and tears and something that can ruins others completely.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Frankenstein (book)\">Frankenstein (book)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects\">A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects</a><ul><li>This is a theme that is present in many of the cases described in this note, as a strict deference to the value of honesty and the safety of the people (Oedipus), or a strict intellectual rigor (Hamlet), or scientific discovery and human technological advancement (Frankenstein) ultimately brings them all down.</li></ul></li></ul>","Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Licensure is, under the vast majority of circumstances, unnecessary.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Today, over 20% of jobs in the US require some form of licensure. This results in a situation where only the incumbents benefit, thus reducing economic competition, and increases prices for consumers, resulting in economic inefficiency.</p><h4>Argument</h4><ul><li>It is the case that many jobs that are dangerous, or would cause dangers to others should be licensed to ensure public safety. However, this does not justify an across the board licensure of many different fields.</li><li>Licensure in many harmless fields only result in the incumbents being kept in power, as they can control the amount of people who receive the license, thus holding an economic monopoly on the supply of some occupation. This results in economic inefficiency.<ul><li>In many cases, the board that determines whether some people would receive the license represents a clear conflict of interest, and by artificially restricting the amount of people that are entering a profession, it represents a sort of democratic backsliding, as the interests of the people are not being represented. </li></ul></li><li>A <a href=\"https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/sites/default/files/docs/licensing_report_final_nonembargo.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2015 study by the White House</a> concluded that most licensing does not results in a higher quality of service provided.</li><li>Licensing should only be used when <strong>necessary, targeted (at specific professions), and competently administered (avoiding conflicts of interest).</strong></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>More licensure could be a part of the reason that this is happening?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>So this is similar to licensure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>A licensure system should be on where only the incumbents benefit, and thus would be a bad system.</li></ul></li></ul>","Meritocracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An essay that describes in broad strokes the arguments and positions relating to the concept of meritocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding the broad topics and arguments surrounding the idea of meritocracy.</p><h4>Historical Survey</h4><p>Meritocracy has a long tradition in East Asia, mainly due to the perceived consequentialist benefits.</p><ul><li>“People will be more virtuous, and better cared-for, if they are ruled by the meritorious.” (Mulligan, 2023)Other thinkers, such as Plato and Aristotle have also participated in the tradition of favoring meritocracy.However, while Plato and Eastern thinkers mainly concerned themselves with meritocracy when it comes to governance, A. deals generally with the distribution of social goods.</li><li>For A. specifically, he believes in proportional equality, meaning that the distribution of social goods is proportional to the merit of the persons.</li><li>He defines merit roughly as how well something to achieves their intended goal.This idea is also related to the idea of the American Dream.</li></ul><h4>Merit</h4><p>Merit is contextual, i.e., whether someone is meritorious depends on the context which defines their merit. Merit is internal, and is distinct from the outcomes which result from the merit.</p><ul><li>We can’t judge someone’s merit merely based on outcomes.We must choose between two definitions of merit:</li><li><strong>Consequentialist</strong>: one chooses meritocracy because of the results it produces; it is only contingently valuable.<ul><li>But the issue is that there might be things that are not related to merit that might produce a better outcome.</li></ul></li><li>\\<em>\\*Categorical: \\</em>\\*one chooses meritocracy because running a system based on merit is inherently good.<ul><li>But the issue then is that you are not always able to produce the best results.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Merit and Desert</h4><p>There is no generally agreed upon definition for the two terms. Desert generally is pre-institutional; it is what people deserve that decides institutions/the rules that govern society. It is a concept that can be used to judge institutions to see of they are fair/good or not.</p><ul><li>A libertarian view would argue that this does not matter, and as long as individuals chose to participate in the institutions, they must abide by the institutions and external considerations of desert are ignored.Desert is also often thought of as having two properties:</li><li>Aboutness: the basis for desert must be “about” the subject of a desert-claim. This is often construed as responsibility.</li><li>Fitness: there is a natural connection between the basis for desert and the object being awarded.</li></ul><h4>Equal Opportunity</h4><p>There is two conceptions of equal opportunity:</p><ul><li>Fair/substantive: people start with the same amount of resources (in the broadest sense of the term) in the initial position.</li><li>Formal: the institutions in society apply equally and fairly to all.A meritocracy is merely a society where there is equality of opportunity in both senses.There are two salient debates when it comes to equal opportunity that is relevant here:</li><li>Whether there is a moral difference between social advantages (being born into a rich family) and natural advantages (being born intelligence).</li><li>The extent to which the state can interfere with family affairs to ensure equal opportunity.“The philosophical issues here are to explain what is problematic and what is not, and to draw the line between the government’s interest in establishing equal opportunity and the liberty of parents to raise their children as they see fit.” (Mulligan, 2023)</li></ul><h4>Governance</h4><p>There are three questions principally related to the idea of governing with a meritocracy:</p><ol><li>How do you define merit?</li><li>How should the meritocrats rule?</li><li>Is meritocracy justifiable?</li></ol><h5>New Confucian Meritocracy</h5><p>These theories are based on a mixture of Western liberal political theory and traditional Chinese Confucian political theory.</p><h5>Restricted Suffrage</h5><p>Another set of theories that are broadly related to the concept of restricting the right to make political decisions to those who are sufficiently competent.</p><h5>Plural Voting</h5><p>A set of theories which argues that people should be given differing amounts of votes based on their competence. This has consequences, such as the fact that:</p><ol><li>Some people with lower allocations of votes would de facto be restricted from suffrage.</li><li>There is no unique assignment of votes (all votes could be multiplied by 2, and the result would be the same).</li></ol><h4>Justice</h4><p>There is obviously an instrumental connection between meritocracy and justice, since many systems of thought see most of the time that putting the most competent in positions of power is the best means of achieving some end. However, the most vocal proponents of meritocracy claim that meritocracy works as a deontological principle as well. One response is to say that since in a meritocracy, people get what they deserve, and getting what one deserves is just, that meritocracy is just. Additionally, meritocracy is intuitively appealing for people, and it is grounded in autonomy, meaning that we consider only the relevant facts (whether someone is able to perform some tasks well) and thus we treat them as agents whose purposeful acts can make a difference in the world (we respect them). Another idea is the the very nature of social goods points to merit based distribution.</p><ul><li>When the goal is to produce bagels, it is inherently (intuitively) true that the person who should get this job is the person who is best at making bagels. The fact that the person who is best at making bagels gets the job is “primitive” and arises as aesthetic experience does.</li></ul><h5>Distribution of Jobs</h5><p>When it comes to a just distribution of jobs, the meritocrat would argue that only merit be considered, and all non-merit factors ought not to be considered. However, it is difficult to ascertain what exactly constitutes a merit factor.</p><ul><li>For instance, does beauty count as a merit factor? If a customer is more likely to buy from a sales agent who is beautiful, should that be a factor of consideration in the hiring process?Affirmative action is a policy that is disliked by meritocrats because it violates the principle of equal opportunity, and is discriminatory.</li></ul><h5>Distribution of Income</h5><p>There are a few arguments made in favor of a meritocratic distribution of income:</p><ul><li>Desert; people who work harder deserve more income.There are multiple ways of measuring economic contribution: WTP, consumer surplus, marginal product.A complication is how to disentangle the contribution of labor vs. capital in the production of something. And it seems that how the capital was acquired is of moral consideration as well.Economic rents is also a problem, since rents are incomes not associated with any sort of economic contribution. For instance:</li><li>Executive self-dealing</li><li>Luck-based income</li><li>Rent-seeking behavior (capturing value without creating it)</li><li>The beauty premium</li></ul><h4>Objections</h4><h5>Rawls</h5><p>Rawls claims that meritocracy is only contingently and instrumentally useful in the case that it furthers the Difference Principle. More specifically, he has four critiques of meritocracy:</p><ol><li>Targeting desert-based theories broadly, he claims that no one “deserves” anything, since all differences between people are either due to social circumstances or natural differences in ability, neither of which is morally relevant.</li><li>He views it as being impossible to accurately track people’s merit and thus distribute things on a basis of merit.</li><li>He thinks that it is possible that meritocracy is the result of comprehensive doctrines, and not the result of bargaining between people. Therefore, it would lack universal justification to serve as a shared political base.’</li><li>It is insufficiently democratic.</li></ol><h5>Relational Egalitarianism</h5><p>Believes that the fundamental principle of society is equality, and since meritocracy violates that principle, it is morally repugnant.</p><h5>Democratic Theory</h5><p>The first group in this camp that rejects meritocracy are the <em>epistemic democrats</em>, who argue that democracy produces good decisions. For instance, they use Condorcet’s Jury Theorem to argue the point of the \"wisdom of the crowd.\" The meritocrat can respond in the following three manners:</p><ul><li>Deny the relevance of the model, since its assumptions are violated in the real world (no independence condition met, or reject that assumption that the average  $p &gt; 0.5$  ).</li><li>Concede that the theorem is true, but that meritocracy does better, since it gains all the benefits of the theory (from aggregation), but suffers none of the downsides.</li><li>Argue that this supports meritocracy, since if one where to give the more informed more votes, the limit of the aggregate probability of the right decisions increases as the amount of voters approaches infinity is faster than in the democratic case.</li></ul><p>$$ \\prod_{n=0}^{\\infty} p_{n} \\ \\text{where} \\ p \\in [0,1] \\text{ and }p_{n} \\text{ is more informed than} \\ p_{n+1} $$</p><pre><code>The above approaches 1 faster than merely:</code></pre><p>$$ \\prod_{n=0}^{\\infty} p_{n} \\ \\text{where} \\ p \\in [0,1] $$</p><pre><code>Assuming that $\\frac{\\sum p_{n}}{n} &gt; 0.5$ in both cases.</code></pre><p>For <em>procedural democrats</em> all that matters is that the principles associated with democracy are carried out, and whether the end effect is better or worse does not matter. <em>Epistemic proceduralism</em> attempts to marry the two schools of thought into one.</p><h5>Libertarianism</h5><p>The meritocratic distributive rule of hiring on the basis of merit violates the libertarian right to contract. Additionally, Hayek gives the critique that a meritocratic economy may not be the most efficient, since letting people choose what they want to do maximizes surplus, while choosing based on merit might not.</p><h4>References</h4>","One has no other obligations except to oneself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of egoism, that there are no other interests one needs to serve other than oneself’s.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core belief of the early Taoists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>From this concept that oneself is the most important thing (人人为自己), we can derive the other important precept of the early Taoists, that of “despising things and valuing life (轻物重生),” since all that one should care about is oneself, and what is more core to oneself than one’s life, one should naturally place one’s life above all else.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Lüshi Chunqiu</em> (吕氏春秋), “The Importance of Self”</p><p>今吾生之为我有，而利我亦大矣。论其贵贱，爵为天子，不足以比焉；论其轻重，富有天下，不可以易之；论其安危，一曙失之，终身不复得。此三者，有道者之所慎也。 The life I possess is truly my own, and its value to me is immense. In terms of worth, even the rank of Son of Heaven cannot compare to it; in terms of weight, not even the wealth of the entire realm could be exchanged for it; in terms of security, once lost at the break of dawn, it can never be regained in a lifetime. These three aspects are matters that the wise treat with the utmost caution.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","Pass-through businesses are taxed once, at the level of the owner’s income":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A pass-through entity is not taxed on its profits directly; the profits are attributed to the owners and taxed as their personal income, so unlike a corporation the business bears no separate layer of tax.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The distinction determines who a change in business taxation actually reaches, since a measure aimed at corporations leaves pass-throughs untouched and a measure aimed at personal income captures them.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The 2017 deduction of twenty per cent of qualified pass-through income was defended on this basis, as an offset to the reduction in the corporate rate that pass-throughs could not benefit from.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same\">Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same</a><ul><li>The incidence principle that determines who bears the difference.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>The schedule that pass-through income is assessed under.</li></ul></li></ul>","Plural voting admits no unique assignment of votes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Schemes that allocate votes in proportion to competence cannot fix the allocation uniquely, since multiplying every allocation by a constant leaves the ordering and the outcome unchanged while altering who is effectively disenfranchised.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an internal defect rather than a moral objection, and it applies to any proposal that weights political power by a measured trait.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The indeterminacy matters because the absolute level does have a consequence at the bottom of the distribution: under some admissible scalings a citizen’s allocation rounds to nothing, so a purely formal rescaling silently converts plural voting into restricted suffrage.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ranked-choice voting system cannot satisfy all four conditions of fairness\">A ranked-choice voting system cannot satisfy all four conditions of fairness</a><ul><li>Another structural impossibility result about voting rules.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law\">Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law</a><ul><li>The objection from the other direction.</li></ul></li></ul>","Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are main three problems with price controls: supply shortages, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These represents the large issues that manifest when price controls are implemented.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Price controls cause:</p><ol><li>Supply shortages.</li><li>Misallocation of resources, since people have to spend time looking for alternatives, waiting in line to get something that they need, and the people that ultimately get something are often not the ones that value the thing the most.</li><li>Rent-seeking, since people will engage in activities such as attempting to bribe the people who are selling the good.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people&#x27;s misunderstanding of what causes the issue\">The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people's misunderstanding of what causes the issue</a><ul><li>Rather than supporting pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis, people believe that rent control is the way to solve the problem, while there are many problems associated with price controls.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a><ul><li>When there are price controls, the market is not able to operate efficiently and accomplish the things it would be able to accomplish otherwise, such as the things listed above.</li></ul></li></ul>","Returns to intelligence are diminishing, though we still have far to go before hitting the asymptote":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Returns to intelligence are diminishing, though it seems that the gains of extra intelligence remains high for now.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines the potential benefits that future AI development could bring for us, while at the same time acknowledging the potential limits.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>That returns to intelligence are diminishing can be explained via a few possible avenues:</p><ul><li>That there is a limited amount of knowledge in existence, and therefore, it becomes harder for the same amount of intelligence to be effective.</li><li>That due to chaos, discovering the marginal piece of knowledge becomes more and more difficult the more we learn.</li></ul><p>There are three things that more intelligence, specifically from AI, could continue to do for us, however:</p><ol><li>Artificial intelligences can be replicated easily, and could potentially be more powerful than human intelligences. Additionally, they are divisible and allow for more precise allocations of compute.</li><li>Artificial intelligences could potentially be better at capturing diffused tacit knowledge (little pieces of knowledge that is distrusted across a process/supply chain), since they are able to centrally process a far larger amount of data, and thus they would be able to optimize many production processes.</li><li>Artificial intelligences could potentially be able to formulate the existence of “fuzzy/cloud laws” whereby the relationship between factors cannot not specifically be described by one deterministic model, necessitating more complex means of modeling in order to see the relationship between multiple variables. AIs have this capacity.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI represents a new epistemological category\">AI represents a new epistemological category</a><ul><li>This is the reason that AI represents such a huge jump in capabilities in so many fields, including in the processes described here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets\">AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets</a><ul><li>Related to its ability to process large amounts of information.</li></ul></li></ul>","The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The July Revolution of 1830 was a revolution against Charles X, due to his poor management. This was due in large to two changes carried out by Charles, namely the <a href=\"https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Anti-Sacrilege_Act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anti-Sacrilege Act</a> and the provision of indemnities for those whose properties were seized during the First French Revolution, in addition to overall poor administration by Charles.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This example showcases the important of ensure that adequate and correct reforms are passed, because otherwise, the people may just rebel.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Yang of Sui&#x27;s excessive spending, disregard for human life, and general poor management led to the collapse of the Sui\">Yang of Sui's excessive spending, disregard for human life, and general poor management led to the collapse of the Sui</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill\">Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill</a></li></ul>","The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Proposes the thesis that capitalism was able to flourish due to what Weber termed the “Protestant Work Ethic.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This book serves as one of the foundational texts of sociology, and proposes an influential thesis that is this impactful today.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement\">The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth\">The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of predestination in Calvinism provides a methodical rationalization of moral life\">The idea of predestination in Calvinism provides a methodical rationalization of moral life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism\">The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement\">The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Reformation did not engender the spirit of capitalism\">The Reformation did not engender the spirit of capitalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul><h4>References'</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Protestant Work Ethics is a work that sought to refute the claims of Economic Determinists\">The Protestant Work Ethics is a work that sought to refute the claims of Economic Determinists</a></li></ul>","The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the existence of evil in the world makes it unlikely that a perfectly loving God actually exists.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the powerful arguments against the existence of God.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h4>In Literature</h4><p>This argument is espoused by Ivan Karamazov from <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The left-wing biases of researchers manifest through their research, as was proven through a study whereby teams of scientists were given the same data on immigration, yet around 2/3 of the researchers found positive outcomes for immigration. Additionally, the personal biases of the researchers correlate positively with the outcome of their research.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that much of the science that comes out of researchers, especially in social science and humanities fields could potentially be biased.</p><h4>Notes</h4><ul><li>This does not regard a particular orientation, since while left-leaning researchers tend to find conclusions that support their political belief, right-leaning researches tend to find conclusions that support their political belief.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American white males are underrepresented in academia and media in lower-level positions, and overrepresented in upper level positions\">American white males are underrepresented in academia and media in lower-level positions, and overrepresented in upper level positions</a><ul><li>In fact, this is the case even in hiring.</li></ul></li></ul>","The consumer chooses to consume the amount that makes the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods equal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is known as the Equimarginal Principle. Due to the law of diminishing marginal utility, when choosing between two goods, we optimize utility by choosing to consume the amount where the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods is the same.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This law allows us to understand how to maximize utility.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This intuitively makes sense because if one of the goods has a higher marginal utility per dollar, than we would shift our consumption from the other good to that good in order to maximize utility. The mathematical formulation of this principle is as follows: $$ \\frac{MU_{x}}{P_{x}}=\\frac{MU_{y}}{P_{y}} $$ This model can actually account for many different things since we can consider one of the goods to be a composite good, i.e., money. Then the mathematical formulation would be as such: $$ \\frac{MU_{x}}{P_{x}}=\\lambda $$ Where $\\lambda$ represents the marginal utility of income.</p><h4>References</h4>","The invention of paper money by the Song allowed for the development of modern economics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The invention of paper money, which replaced much heavier coin money, allowed for the easier transport of value across vast distances, making trade easier. Additionally, it no longer meant that the production of units of value was tied to the rarity of the intrinsic material.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The invention of paper money is one of the most important developments in economics, and allowed for the development of advanced economics in the future.</p><h4>References</h4>","The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The institution of the family is crucial for the prospering of liberal ideals in a society.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>While many argue that the institution of the family is incompatible with the principles of liberalism, this note says otherwise.</p><h4>Explanations</h4><ul><li>Family is where humans first learn about rules and rule-making. They also learn about decreasing their time preference, which is a crucial component in being able to participate in the free market.</li><li>Family members have genuine information about the state/nature of people in their family, and are thus knowledge-process institutions which give far more granularity then if the state were to attempt such a thing.</li><li>Families can train their children to have the liberal ethos, which produce people who act in the same way in society.</li><li>The principle of subsidiarity finds its most natural representation in the family. </li><li>Cultural liberalism likewise originates from the family, and attempting to enforce a single model of the family, whether left or right wing, threatens the diversity which undergirds liberal societies.</li><li>Family teaches us epistemic humility, since we are forced to be with different people that we did not choose.</li><li>Importantly, defending the concept of family is not the same as defending a particular conception of the family.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a><ul><li>What allows for the cultural that allows constitutional liberalism to exist, may in part be due to the family.</li></ul></li></ul>","The state ought to prevent childbirth in cases where the parents would not be able to support the child":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The state has the authority to prevent the birth of children to parents who don’t have the ability to take care of the child.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that Mill advocates for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Although this might prevent the birth of some children, who would otherwise not exist, this policy would, under any widely accepted conception of liberalism, violate core liberal principles of non-intervention and personal liberties. That being said, Mill probably justified this in that this policy protects the rights/liberties of the child, which cannot protect its own rights/liberties before it is born.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth\">Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth</a><ul><li>This is a potential counterargument to this claim.</li></ul></li></ul>","To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the meaninglessness of the universe is best dealt with through acceptance, through the realization of the fact that one is not alone since the universe itself is meaningless and absurd.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the central concept in Absurdism.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Often times, people can have a passive acceptance of the absurd, but it is an active embrace of the absurd which Camus is arguing for here, something that is more profound.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a><ul><li>The Absurdist would argue that such meaning does not actually exist.</li></ul></li></ul>","Truths which are important to society ought to be the ones exempt from skepticism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something which people often, if not explicitly, then implicitly uphold, is the fact that we should not be able to have discussion/skepticism towards facts which hold societal importance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a point of view which Mill addresses and rebutes.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, the argument is referring to beliefs which are indispensable to the well-being of society, such as a fundamental moral value that underlies the legal system of a society, or some other lynchpin of the societal cultural/moral zeitgeist.</p><ul><li>As Mill says, it is often thought that only “bad men” would seek to overturn these values fundamental to society.</li><li>This argument is fundamentally a pragmatist one; what is useful for society is what is “true.” People who support this view reject a search for the real, metaphysical “truth.”<ul><li>But it is the case that the very claim that some idea is “useful” to society is itself a personal judgment that could be wrong.</li></ul></li><li>To know for sure that some idea is so sacred to the fabric of society that we must restrict attempts to discuss alternatives is to assume infallibility.</li></ul><p>This is the means by which Socrates was condemned to die.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>Because people might deem some idea to be so central to society that one should not be able to challenge it, that leads to this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","We should pursue autonomous AI governance rather than AI containment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A better strategy for managing agents is not containment, which fails as computational abilities skyrocket, but the creation of institutional regulations allow for more effective management of agents.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues for a significantly different strategy towards governing AI.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In this framework, the humans are the ultimate constitutional authority and give the essence of the law for the AI agents, who build an administration that specifies and enforces the rules. There is no axiomatic argument to prove that agents will tend to comply with the rules, but observation from existing tests and also human societies in general argue that this would be the case.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems to me that this anthropomorphizes agents too much, since they do not seem to hold many of the same moral values that humans do. The current success of this system may be a result of the fact that AIs act in imitation of humans, but this might vary with future models.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation\">Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation</a><ul><li>Perhaps in specific circumstances, such as high risk scientific research, agents would have the best incentive to work with a bounded framework?</li><li>But at the same time, maybe not, since that would constrain their ability to make breakthroughs.</li></ul></li></ul>","When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the government is unwilling to enact reform that would appease the people, the people may simply take the matter into their own hands and revolt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has been the cause of many revolution in history, and could be an important framework through which to analyze political situations that currently exist and will exist.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms\">The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms</a><ul><li>An example of the principle described in this note in action.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill\">Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill</a><ul><li>An example of the principle described in this note in action.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians\">The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians</a><ul><li>An example of the principle described in this note in action.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power\">The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power</a><ul><li>An elaboration of when people do rise up in revolt, how the government deals with the revolt is also important.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions\">Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions</a></li></ul>"});
